  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 455 AM MDT Fri Oct 2 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nA zonal/westerly flow aloft across the Northern Rockies and MT will \nsupport warming and drying across the area today as primarily mid-\nhigh level cloud-cover thins this afternoon with most lower \nelevation locations reaching maximum temperatures in the 70s. Breezy \nwest to southwest winds are expected this afternoon thanks to deeper \natmospheric mixing while surface troughing also develops in response \nto an approaching weak upper level shortwave disturbance that tracks \neast along the US/Canadian border tonight. A somewhat cooler \nairmass with lighter winds and surface high pressure slides across\nthe area Saturday behind the shortwave. \n\nUpper level ridging amplifies across the SW US later this weekend \nthrough early next week, providing an extended period of above \naverage warmth and dryness with temperatures peaking 15-20F above \nseasonal averages on Monday. A flatter zonal flow redevelops mid-\nlate next week for continued dryness and mild temperatures before \npotential upper level troughing moves in next weekend. \n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nThe warmest and driest conditions of this forecast period are likely \nto occur on Monday with afternoon temperatures as warm as the lower \nto mid 80s which would be within a few degrees of daily records \nat a few locations. In addition, breezy to locally windy \nconditions are likely Monday afternoon, which combined with the \nassociated warmth/dryness could elevate fire weather concerns for \nsome areas. Hoenisch",
